well we were just speaking to new  Griffith about Remembrance Sunday there  and some his Labour's plans to help  veterans and to muck event today we had  the chance to you sit down this week for  a rare interview with the chief and the  defense staff Senate Carter I was in  Grumman's Bay at the time so my  colleague asked a bunghole our defense  and security correspondent went along  and with the NATO summit to be held in  London next month he began the interview  by asking Gemma caster about the  importance of NATO I think in many ways  it's never been as important in its 70  years of its life because the threats  are very diverse now we talk a lot about  Russia but of course what we also need  to be conscious of is that there are  threats to NATO's southern flank from  the way Africa is evolving from  migration and of course from terrorism  as well so this year we signed off a new  NATO military strategy the first time  since 1967 and that's a strategy that  does try and take a 360 degree look at  the problems that Europe has to confront  in security terms so I think in terms of  the Alliance it's probably as important  as it's ever been  do you think it's being undermined at  the edges whether that's through a u.s.  president who criticizes member states  for not spending enough whether it's  turkey for buying Russian equipment for  example now Adair thinks I think there  are always tensions in alliances it's  the same as a marriage and the plain  fact is with 29 nations it's very  important that we all come together and  we all contribute to that alliance in a  fair way so I think it's right that  burden-sharing is on the agenda and that  people aspire to spend the two percent  of their GDP and the 20 percent of that  on their equipment that was signed up to  in Wales in 2014 let me read you a quote  from that precise moment Wales 2014 come  the end of the Cold War in 1990 that  should have been the time for NATO to  shut up shop give up go home and go away  well of course we all had a huge amount  of optimism in 1989 as the wall came  down and we've celebrating that of  course over the last couple of weeks but  the reality is that the world has  changed it's moved on we now have what I  think would be characterized as global  competition between great powers  something that we probably haven't seen  of this ilk since the middle and the  early part of the last century  so I think NATO's relevance is now back  where it was during the Cold War because  of the nature of that threat in that  evolving global strategic context the  quote I read you is said by Jeremy  Corbyn it could be Prime Minister in a  few weeks time presumably you therefore  entirely disagree with his opinion on  NATO well you wouldn't expect me during  an election campaign to make any  observations about politics at all my  job is to provide a military advice to  whoever will be the leader of the day  entirely however if you feel that he in  any way jeopardize the national security  of this country I would perhaps expect  you to make that clear as people go to  the polls my job is to serve whoever is  the leader of the day and to provide the  appropriate military advice when they  ask me to do that but I've asked you  about NATO in your support of NATO and  you clearly have a very different view  on NATO to Jeremy Corbyn I don't know  it's a straight answer but my view as a  military adviser to any leader of this  country is that NATO remains a very  important part of our national security  and it's right and proper that we should  lean into NATO in every way possible can  I ask you about the IOC's report into  Russian interference I ask you only  because you sit on the National Security  Council I mean do you have an opinion on  whether or not that should have been  released given that it was cleared by  the intelligence agencies no because  once again this is a political question  and it's the choice of the prime  minister as to whether he published this  report at the moment the reports that I  understand is still going through a  process and I have not yet seen it  myself that's then turned to the map  behind you d-day this year we mark the  70th anniversary of that event it was a  opportune moment to remember the  selflessness and the heroism of those  who went across to northern France on  that moment yes certainly and of course  some that's why remembrance matters to  us as well and I think quite  anniversaries like d-day do for us is  they provide us with an opportunity to  educate people because I think it is the  fact these days that fewer people  probably study history or certainly no  history and on the back of that I think  anniversaries are a chance to bring some  of that to life and of course what's  interesting about d-day is we always  think rightly I'm sure about the bravery  of those who stormed the beaches and to  enable the operation from the sea  from the air but actually the  interesting thing about d-day was really  it was a national enterprise it was an  enterprise where you know most of  society got behind it and scientists  businessman railway engineers people who  understood programming and that sort of  detail all came together to provide some  really quite ingenious solutions to what  were really quite difficult problems to  solve and that notion of a national  enterprise I think he's relevant to some  of the problems and challenges that we  have today and certainly in terms of how  our armed forces need to be supported  and what we need to do as we modernize  definitely requires us to think hard  about how we mobilize the effort not  just at the private sector but of  society as a whole do you think it's a  national characteristic that we are  lacking at the moment I wouldn't say  that I think we're living through a  period of phenomenal change at the  moment it's changed it is probably more  more enormous than even the two world  wars combined  and it's also the pace of it is is  increasing rapidly  and on the basis of that I think we do  need to think pretty hard about how as a  nation we're going to manage that change  and it matters to our armed forces  hugely is there a lesson though is there  anything we can take from those those  men and women who performed such heroic  actions in order to liberate Europe on  that day well and I think the lesson  I've taken from it is that we need to  avail ourselves of all of the skills in  art and talent in our society in order  to deal with today's problems and that  requires I think a much more integrated  approach across all of our national  effort how would you assess the British  approach to the UM forces at the moment  given where we are in terms of our  engagement around the world well I can't  remember time in certainly in my  lifetime when the Armed Forces were more  popular and we polled a parent or an  approval rating of gusting 90 percent  last year I think the risk of course  there with the unpopular campaigns in  the last 10 to 15 years is that that  popularity can often be based on  sympathy and not on empathy and of  course our veterans and our armed forces  they don't want to be pitied because  they serve what they actually want is  they want to be respected so I think in  terms of our relationship with society  we very much like it to be based upon  respect and I think a lot of  understand that but I get back to my  point about education it's important  that we continue to educate people on  that the risk of drugging you again into  politics the armed forces have global  projection what role could you therefore  play in a post brexit world in order to  try and fulfill government's agenda of  having a global Britain well I think I  think we need to have a conversation as  a nation about what sort of nation we  want to be in the future and of course  what the Armed Forces offer in support  of our foreign security policy is the  ability to have a presence globally and  we do that in all sorts of ways I mean  at the moment it may surprise people to  know that we've had people in some 65  different countries this year either  providing training or indeed operational  input to what's occurring we do that  through our Navy of course which is its  most obvious in body meant of global but  we also do it through our Air Force and  also through our army so there's a great  deal that we can do globally in order to  be able to promote our values promote  our influence and to project image and  indeed to promote our prosperity and I  think the Armed Forces is an  extraordinarily good way of achieving  that because the values that we espouse  are the values that many of our allies  also wish to espouse tuefel defend stuff  thank you very much  Thanks

